-- =====================================================================
-- Level 3 Listening story bank.
--
-- 16 briefings of about two and a half minutes, each with 10 questions.
--
-- WHAT MAKES THIS LEVEL 3, AND NOT A HARDER LEVEL 2. Every Level 1 and Level 2 story is ONE
-- situation. Every story here carries TWO migrations running at the same time for the same
-- customer -- a Message migration and a Content migration, a Mail cutover and a Shared Drive
-- move -- and the questions are written so that a candidate who followed only one of them
-- cannot answer. Several items ask which of the two an event belonged to; several ask a
-- comparison that exists in neither scenario alone. Holding two threads apart while listening
-- once is the skill this level tests.
--
-- The combinations, and the vocabulary that goes with them, are taken from the migration
-- documentation tool: Message (Slack to Teams, Slack to Chat, Teams to Chat, Chat to Teams and
-- the same-platform moves), Mail (Outlook and Gmail in all four directions) and Content (Shared
-- Drive, SPO and OneDrive in all nine). Terminology is real and current for each: channels,
-- direct messages, threads, reactions, pinned posts and message fidelity on the Message side;
-- mailboxes, shared mailboxes, aliases, labels against folders, delegates and MX cutover on the
-- Mail side; document libraries, permissions, sharing links, version history, path length and
-- native-format conversion on the Content side.
--
-- US PROJECT LANGUAGE is deliberate and consistent, because it is what these candidates hear on
-- customer calls: kickoff, statement of work, change request, dry run, UAT, go-live, cutover
-- window, blackout period, sign-off, punch list, hypercare, escalation path, service level
-- agreement, root cause analysis, run book, stakeholder, checkpoint call, staging. Each is
-- named plainly enough to be learned from context. What is NOT here is office idiom -- no
-- boiling the ocean, no kicking tires. That was tried at Level 2 and removed: it tested
-- vocabulary nobody had been taught rather than English anybody needed.
--
-- Question design, per story: item 1 is the cause of the FIRST scenario and item 2 the cause of
-- the SECOND, each with the plausible wrong hypothesis from the narrative among the options; at
-- least two items require the two scenarios to be compared or told apart; one is arithmetic
-- across both; one separates what was DEFERRED from what was RESOLVED; the last asks which
-- sentence best summarises the whole briefing, where the wrong options are true of one scenario
-- but not of both.
--
-- QUESTIONS TEST COMPREHENSION, NOT MEMORY. An early cut asked for numbers and names stated once
-- in the briefing -- how many documents, how long the vendor took, what an option cost -- which a
-- candidate answers from short-term memory rather than from having followed anything. Those were
-- replaced: every question now turns on implication, trade-off, consequence or the speakers own
-- judgement, and its answer is present in the briefing without being a sentence to recall. The one
-- arithmetic item that survives spans BOTH migrations, so it is a calculation rather than a recall.
--
-- Seeded behind a seed_state marker. Bump the key to push a revision, and delete the old rows
-- first -- a title that already exists would otherwise attach its questions to the wrong story.
--
-- Avoid apostrophes: these are single-quoted SQL literals.
-- =====================================================================

-- ---------- Stories 1 to 4 ----------
DELETE FROM listening_question WHERE story_id IN (SELECT id FROM listening_story WHERE level = 3)
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l3-listening-bank-v3');
DELETE FROM listening_story WHERE level = 3
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l3-listening-bank-v3');

INSERT INTO listening_story (created_at, updated_at, title, script, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), v.title, v.script, 3, 'HARD'
FROM (VALUES
    ('Ardent Financial: Two Cutovers, One Weekend',
     'Ardent Financial ran two migrations across the same weekend. The first was Message: eleven hundred users moving from Slack to Teams, with four thousand channels and nine years of direct messages. The second was Content: sixty terabytes moving from Shared Drive to SPO for the same population. Programme director Yvonne Castellanos had insisted the two run together, against advice, because Ardent had negotiated a single blackout period with its regulator and a second one would not be granted for nine months. The Message side finished early. Channels, threads, reactions and pinned posts all carried across at ninety-nine point four percent fidelity, and the only real casualty was the app integrations: forty-one Slack bots had no Teams equivalent and were listed on the punch list rather than replaced. The Content side is where the weekend was lost. Throughput on the document libraries collapsed on Saturday afternoon and engineer Marcus Feld reported that SPO was throttling the tenant, recommending they wait it out. Analyst Ruth Ijeoma checked the connector logs instead and found the cause was on the Shared Drive side: a single drive holding two hundred and twenty thousand files in one flat folder was being re-enumerated on every pass. They excluded it, recovered full throughput inside the hour, and pre-staged that drive separately. What was deferred rather than solved was sharing: eighteen thousand anyone-with-the-link URLs were converted to company-only, which Ardent accepted for internal content and refused for the client-facing library, so that library still carries open links today with no owner assigned to review them. The weekend closed at four on Sunday morning, ninety minutes inside the window. The Message migration came in eleven thousand dollars under its statement of work, and the Content migration ran nineteen thousand over, because the weekend engineering that recovered the throughput was billable and Ardent approved it in writing at two in the morning.'),

    ('Brightline Health: Mail Late, Messages Early',
     'Brightline Health moved from Gmail to Outlook for two thousand four hundred mailboxes, and at the same time from Chat to Teams for the same staff. Migration manager Aditi Raghavan planned the Mail cutover for the first weekend of May and the Message cutover for the third, deliberately two weeks apart so the helpdesk would not face both at once. It was the Message migration that arrived early and the Mail one that arrived late, and neither was for the reason anyone expected. Chat to Teams finished four days ahead because Brightline had almost no group direct messages -- clinicians communicate in shared channels by policy -- and group DMs are the slowest object in that migration. Mail slipped by six days, and the cause was not volume. It was labels. Gmail labels are not folders: a message carrying five labels appears in five places, and Brightline averaged three point four labels per message across eleven million messages. The mapping to Outlook folders had been sized as though each message landed once. Engineer Tomas Bergstrom proposed dropping to a single primary label per message, which would have finished on time and lost the clinicians their filing. Aditi refused, on the grounds that a mailbox nobody can search is not a migrated mailbox, and took the six days. Two items went to the punch list. Out-of-office messages do not migrate and had to be reset by each user, which was communicated but not enforced, so several hundred were still silent in June. And nine shared mailboxes kept their delegates only after those delegates signed in once -- described in the run book, not in the user notice. Hypercare ran for two weeks after each cutover rather than one, at a cost Brightline absorbed as part of the change request.'),

    ('Cardinal Freight: The Drive That Was Not In Scope',
     'Cardinal Freight signed a statement of work covering two moves: OneDrive to SPO for four hundred users, and Slack to Chat for the same four hundred. The Content side was straightforward and the Message side was not, but the incident that defined the project belonged to neither. During the dry run, consultant Nadia Osei found a Shared Drive holding eleven terabytes of freight documentation that appeared in no scoping document, no discovery report and no line of the statement of work. It was owned by an operations team that had not been in the kickoff call. Cardinal assumed it would simply be added. Nadia explained why that was not simple: the statement of work had been priced on OneDrive personal storage, where permissions are per user, and a Shared Drive carries group-level permissions that must be mapped to SPO groups one by one. Adding it was a change request, eleven days and forty-eight thousand dollars, not a scope clarification. The Message migration ran in parallel and threw its own problem. Slack to Chat does not carry message formatting the way Slack to Teams does, and Cardinal had a decade of runbooks written as formatted Slack posts. Those arrived as plain text with the structure gone. The team had flagged this as an out-of-scope feature at kickoff, in writing, and nobody at Cardinal had read past the summary page. Both matters went to the steering committee together. The Shared Drive was approved as a change request and delivered in the second phase; the formatting loss was accepted, because re-authoring nine hundred runbooks cost more than living with them. Cardinal signed off eight days later than planned. In the post-mortem Nadia recorded one action: the out-of-scope list must be read aloud at kickoff, not attached to it.'),

    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards',
     'Delmar Logistics had two migrations in flight. Outlook to Outlook, a tenant-to-tenant move of nine hundred mailboxes following an acquisition, and SPO to SPO for the same acquisition, sixteen terabytes across two hundred site collections. Both were designed with a delta pass a week after the main copy, which is standard. On the SPO side the delta ran clean. On the Mail side it did damage. For six days after the main copy, Delmar had left the source tenant writable at their own request, so that a finance group could close the quarter where they were used to working. Forty people did exactly that. The delta faithfully carried those source mailbox changes forward, and in nineteen mailboxes it overwrote newer items that had been created in the destination. Nothing was lost -- every overwritten item was recoverable and all nineteen were restored inside four hours -- but the finance director asked the reasonable question, and the answer was uncomfortable. The request to leave the source writable had been granted by email by one engineer, and the delta had been scheduled by another, and no step in the process required those two people to meet. Engineer Priya Rensselaer drew the distinction that the team adopted: the writable source was a legitimate business decision, and the fault was that a decision and a schedule were allowed to exist without knowing about each other. Two changes followed. A writable source now blocks a delta from being scheduled until it is explicitly acknowledged, and Delmar agreed a hard read-only date in the plan itself, which they had refused twice before. The SPO side, meanwhile, finished two days early and its only punch-list item was version history: only current versions moved under the agreed plan, which the design had stated and the site owners had not expected.')
    ) AS v(title, script)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l3-listening-bank-v3');

INSERT INTO listening_question (created_at, updated_at, story_id, ordinal, question_text,
                                option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option)
SELECT now(), now(), s.id, v.ordinal, v.q, v.a, v.b, v.c, v.d, v.correct
FROM (VALUES
    ('Ardent Financial: Two Cutovers, One Weekend', 1, 'What actually caused the Content throughput to collapse?',
     'SPO throttling the destination tenant',
     'One Shared Drive folder holding 220,000 files being re-enumerated every pass',
     'The 18,000 sharing links being converted',
     'The Message migration competing for bandwidth', 'B'),
