     'Channel membership had to be rebuilt by hand',
     'Reactions and pinned posts were lost', 'B'),
    ('Cardinal Freight: The Drive That Was Not In Scope', 4, 'How had that formatting loss been handled beforehand?',
     'It was flagged in writing as out of scope at kickoff',
     'It was covered by the change request',
     'It was raised only after the dry run',
     'It had not been identified', 'A'),
    ('Cardinal Freight: The Drive That Was Not In Scope', 5, 'What does treating the extra drive as a change request rather than a clarification reveal about the original price?',
     'It assumed the operations team would attend the kickoff call',
     'It had been priced too low across the whole engagement',
     'It excluded any content discovered after kickoff, whatever its type',
     'It had been priced on a permissions model that the new drive did not share', 'D'),
    ('Cardinal Freight: The Drive That Was Not In Scope', 6, 'How did the steering committee decide on the two matters?',
     'Rejected the drive and re-authored the runbooks',
     'Approved both as change requests',
     'Approved the drive as a change request; accepted the formatting loss',
     'Deferred both to a second phase', 'C'),
    ('Cardinal Freight: The Drive That Was Not In Scope', 7, 'Why was living with the formatting loss the cheaper option?',
     'The runbooks were rarely used',
     'Chat could not display formatting at all',
     'Re-authoring nine hundred runbooks cost more than accepting it',
     'The operations team preferred plain text', 'C'),
    ('Cardinal Freight: The Drive That Was Not In Scope', 8, 'What do the two problems have in common?',
     'Both were things the customer had been told but had not taken in',
     'Both were caused by the migration tooling',
     'Both were discovered after go-live',
     'Both were resolved by change requests', 'A'),
    ('Cardinal Freight: The Drive That Was Not In Scope', 9, 'What does Nadia post-mortem action address?',
     'The pricing model for Shared Drives',
     'How out-of-scope items are communicated, not what they are',
     'Who is invited to a dry run',
     'The formatting limits of Slack to Chat', 'B'),
    ('Cardinal Freight: The Drive That Was Not In Scope', 10, 'Which sentence best summarises the whole briefing?',
     'A message migration that lost formatting because of a tooling defect',
     'Two migrations where both surprises were failures of reading rather than of engineering',
     'Two migrations delayed by an unscoped Shared Drive',
     'A project that overran because the steering committee could not decide', 'B'),
    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards', 1, 'What caused nineteen mailboxes to lose newer items?',
     'A fault in the tenant-to-tenant migration tool',
     'A delta pass carrying forward changes from a source that was still writable',
     'Users restoring older items by mistake',
     'The SPO delta running against the wrong site collection', 'B'),
    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards', 2, 'Why was the source left writable?',
     'It had been overlooked at cutover',
     'Delmar asked for it so a finance group could close the quarter',
     'The delta pass required it',
     'The acquisition agreement demanded it', 'B'),
    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards', 3, 'What was actually lost?',
     'Nothing: every overwritten item was recoverable',
     'Six days of finance work',
     'Version history on the SPO side',
     'Nineteen mailboxes', 'A'),
    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards', 4, 'What distinction did Priya draw?',
     'Between a tool fault and a user fault',
     'Between the Mail and SPO deltas',
     'Between the writable source, which was legitimate, and a decision and a schedule not knowing about each other',
     'Between recoverable and unrecoverable items', 'C'),
    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards', 5, 'Which change addresses the process rather than the symptom?',
     'A writable source now blocks a delta from being scheduled until acknowledged',
     'Restoring the nineteen mailboxes',
     'Telling finance to work in the destination',
     'Running the SPO delta first', 'A'),
    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards', 6, 'What had Delmar refused twice before and now agreed?',
     'Paying for the restoration',
     'A delta pass at all',
     'A hard read-only date in the plan',
     'A tenant-to-tenant migration', 'C'),
    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards', 7, 'What was the only punch-list item on the SPO side?',
     'Site collection permissions',
     'Only current versions moved, which site owners had not expected',
     'Two hundred site collections were miscounted',
     'The delta pass ran late', 'B'),
    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards', 8, 'How did the two deltas differ in outcome?',
     'The SPO delta was cancelled and the Mail delta ran',
     'Both overwrote newer items',
     'The SPO delta ran clean; the Mail delta overwrote newer items',
     'The Mail delta ran clean and the SPO delta failed', 'C'),
    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards', 9, 'What does the SPO version-history item most reasonably illustrate?',
     'Site owners should approve the design',
     'The design was wrong',
     'Version history should always be migrated',
     'Something can be correctly designed, correctly delivered and still surprise the people who use it', 'D'),
    ('Delmar Logistics: The Delta That Went Backwards', 10, 'Which sentence best summarises the whole briefing?',
     'Two deltas that both overwrote newer content',
     'An SPO migration that finished early despite a delta failure',
     'A mail migration that lost nineteen mailboxes after an acquisition',
     'Two deltas from one acquisition: one uneventful, one that did damage because two correct decisions never met', 'D')
    ) AS v(story_title, ordinal, q, a, b, c, d, correct)
JOIN listening_story s ON s.level = 3 AND s.title = v.story_title
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l3-listening-bank-v3');

-- ---------- Stories 5 to 8 ----------
INSERT INTO listening_story (created_at, updated_at, title, script, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), v.title, v.script, 3, 'HARD'
FROM (VALUES
    ('Ferro Manufacturing: The Alias Nobody Owned',
     'When Ferro Manufacturing acquired a competitor, two migrations followed. Gmail to Outlook for six hundred acquired staff, and Teams to Teams, a tenant-to-tenant consolidation of both companies channels. Architect Lena Vogel sequenced them so that Mail went first, because a Teams tenant move depends on the identities the Mail move creates. The Mail side hit a problem that was neither volume nor labels. Ferro and the acquired company both used the same corporate suffix on their mail domain, and a domain can live in exactly one tenant. Removing it from the source broke mail for six hundred people for as long as the gap lasted, and Lena could not get that gap below four hours. Finance director Paolo Renzi wanted it done in one night. Lena proposed the opposite: move the six hundred users first onto a temporary routing address, let them work for two weeks, and move the domain only when almost nobody was still behind it. Paolo objected that clients would see an unfamiliar address for a fortnight. Lena answered with a measurement rather than an argument: eleven percent of outbound mail went to external clients, and ninety percent of that went to twenty-six regular contacts who could be told directly. Her plan was approved and the four-hour gap eventually affected six mailboxes instead of six hundred. The Teams side then produced its own surprise. Channel membership migrated cleanly, but forty guest accounts belonging to the acquired company suppliers did not: guest identities are issued by the destination tenant and must be re-invited, and each invitation looks like a phishing message to a supplier who was never told. Ferro sent a signed notice from its own procurement team first, then re-invited in waves. Two items were left open. Seventeen distribution lists were recreated by hand and never reconciled against the originals, and a shared mailbox called Warranty Claims retained its delegates only after each delegate signed in once -- three of them had not signed in by the closure meeting.'),

    ('Granite Partners: Consolidation on Both Sides',
     'Granite Partners is an architecture practice that merged three offices, and it ran two consolidations at once. SPO to OneDrive, moving eight hundred personal work areas out of departmental sites where they had accumulated wrongly, and Slack to Slack, folding three separate workspaces into one. Both were about cleaning up rather than moving platforms, and both were harder than a platform move for that reason. On the Content side the obstacle was path length. Files that had lived four folders deep inside a site collection went five deep in a personal area, and eleven thousand of them breached the path-length limit at the destination. Engineer Callum Reyes wanted to truncate the folder names automatically. Practice manager Ingrid Sorensen refused: the folder names are project codes and a truncated code is a lost drawing. Instead they flattened by one level, which fixed nine thousand of the eleven, and the remaining two thousand were renamed by the project teams themselves against a list. That took eleven working days of the customer own time, which nobody had planned for and which does not appear in any invoice. On the Message side, the three Slack workspaces had overlapping channel names -- each office had a general, a projects and a site-issues channel -- and Slack to Slack cannot merge two channels into one. They had to be migrated as separate channels with a suffix and then archived or merged manually afterwards. Granite chose to keep all three of each and let them die naturally, which the team advised against and recorded as accepted risk. The consolidation completed on schedule. In the post-mortem Ingrid made one observation that Callum wrote into the run book: on a same-platform consolidation, the technical work is easy and the naming decisions are the project, so the naming decisions should be taken before the kickoff call rather than during the dry run.'),

    ('Halloway Media: Going the Other Way',
     'Halloway Media moved in the direction most suppliers rarely see: Outlook to Gmail for nine hundred staff, and Teams to Chat for the same population, after a group-wide decision to standardise on Google. Migration lead Farid Haddad had run the reverse of both many times and warned the steering committee that experience does not simply reverse. On the Mail side the losses land differently. Folders map to labels without difficulty, but Outlook rules do not migrate to Gmail filters, and Halloway staff averaged nine rules each. Nine hundred people rebuilding nine rules is eight thousand small tasks that no project plan contains. Farid did not try to automate it. He had the top twenty rules across the company identified, published as a one-page recipe, and accepted that the long tail would be rebuilt by people as they missed them. On the Message side the loss was formatting and threading depth: Teams replies inside a channel post map into Chat threads unevenly, and code blocks -- which a media engineering team uses constantly -- arrive as plain text. That was declared out of scope at kickoff and read aloud, following the practice adopted after an earlier project where an out-of-scope list was attached and never read. The unexpected problem was neither of these. It was calendar. Recurring meetings with an end date migrate cleanly; recurring meetings with no end date and an exception -- one moved occurrence in a weekly series -- produced duplicates in about four percent of series. Fixing them is manual. Halloway accepted three hundred and forty duplicates as a punch-list item, cleared two hundred within hypercare, and closed the project with the rest still open and an owner named in the customer own IT team. Farid recorded that his warning to the steering committee had been correct in principle and wrong in detail: the reverse direction was harder, but not in the places he had predicted.'),

    ('Ironvale Utilities: Retention on Two Fronts',
     'Ironvale Utilities operates under a regulator that requires certain records to be held for twenty-five years, and it ran two migrations under that obligation at the same time: Shared Drive to OneDrive for eleven hundred staff, and Chat to Chat, a tenant consolidation of its two regional operating companies. Compliance officer Marta Reyes was the decisive voice on both. On the Content side the trap is well known to the team and invisible to everyone else. Retention labels travel with a document, but the RULES behind those labels are configured in the destination, and a label with no rule behind it is decorative. Two point one million documents carried labels. Migrated before the rules were rebuilt, every one of them would have arrived looking correctly classified and behaving as though it were not classified at all -- nothing on any screen would look wrong. Rebuilding the rules first added five weeks. Engineer Ben Kirchner proposed migrating the seven-year content first while the twenty-five-year rules were built in parallel, which would have recovered three of those weeks. Marta rejected it, and her reason was not technical: an inspector who found any document in an unclassified state would question the entire programme, and that conversation costs more than five weeks. On the Message side the same principle produced the opposite answer. Chat messages under legal hold cannot be migrated while the hold is active, and Ironvale had four holds covering roughly nine thousand messages. Marta did not delay the migration for them. Those conversations stayed in the source tenant, which remains licensed for two more years at eleven thousand dollars a year, and the rest moved on schedule. Asked why she blocked one migration and let the other proceed, she gave a one-line answer that went into the run book: a rule you cannot evidence is a risk, and a message you deliberately left behind is a record.')
    ) AS v(title, script)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l3-listening-bank-v3');

