-- ---------- Stories 9 to 12 ----------
INSERT INTO listening_story (created_at, updated_at, title, script, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), v.title, v.script, 3, 'HARD'
FROM (VALUES
    ('Juniper Retail: Inside the Freeze',
     'Juniper Retail operates a change freeze from the twentieth of November to the fifth of January, and in September its programme manager Osei Danquah discovered that two migrations had been scheduled straight through it. Chat to Teams for fourteen hundred store and head-office staff, and OneDrive to OneDrive, a tenant-to-tenant move of the same peoples personal work areas following a group restructure. The freeze protects the trading period and does not move; the last exception had been granted in a year when a payment system failed. Osei brought three options to the customer. Complete both before the twentieth of November, which required starting in the first week of October and depended on an account clean-up Juniper had not begun. Split them, running Messages before the freeze and Content in January, which meant four hundred licences held for two extra months. Or compress both into six waves instead of ten, which raises the weekly user count from a hundred and forty to two hundred and thirty and, on the desks own figures, raises helpdesk load more than proportionally. IT director Fiona Marsh chose the first and made the clean-up her own commitment, delivering it in eleven days rather than the four weeks originally scheduled, by pulling two people off another project. Both migrations completed on the fourteenth of December. There was one genuine failure inside that success. User acceptance testing for the Message side was signed off by a group of eleven head-office staff, none of whom worked in a store, and stores use Chat spaces differently -- shift handovers are posted as threaded replies to a single daily message. Nobody tested that pattern, and threaded handovers arrived in Teams flattened into a single channel post. It was reported on the first Monday of the rollout by a store manager, not by the project. The fix was a re-migration of eleven spaces. Osei recorded the cost of the two borrowed people, which fell on a project outside his own reporting, on the grounds that a plan that works by moving cost into somebody else column should say so.'),

    ('Kestrel Insurance: What the Regulator Can See',
     'Kestrel Insurance ran Slack to Chat for nine hundred staff and SPO to Shared Drive for its claims archive, both under the eye of a regulator that can demand any communication or document within ten working days. Compliance lead Douglas Whyte set one requirement over both: whatever happens, an e-discovery search run after the migration must return the same result as one run before it. That requirement did more work than any technical constraint. On the Message side it meant direct messages had to migrate, which is the slowest and most sensitive part of a Slack to Chat move, and it meant timestamps had to be preserved exactly, because a search by date range that shifts by even a day is not the same search. Migrated messages arrive with a posted-as attribution and their original timestamp in the body rather than the header, which is normal and which Douglas had to explain to his own legal team twice. On the Content side it meant the claims archive kept its version history, which doubled the volume from nine terabytes to nineteen and pushed the project three weeks longer. Engineer Sofia Bianchi asked whether current versions would do. Douglas answered that a claims file without its revisions is evidence of the outcome and not of the decision, and that the regulator asks about decisions. External sharing was the item that could not be preserved. Forty loss adjusters outside the company held links into the SPO library, and a Shared Drive shares by account rather than by link. Every adjuster had to be added as a member, which needed each firm to nominate named individuals, which took five weeks and finished after go-live. Kestrel ran the two systems side by side for that period, at a licence cost of nine thousand dollars, and Douglas signed a note into the run book: preserve the search, price the sharing, and never promise both on the same date.'),

    ('Lattimer Group: Same Platform, Different Tenant',
     'Lattimer Group is a holding company that absorbed one of its own subsidiaries, and both of its migrations were same-platform tenant moves: Gmail to Gmail for seven hundred mailboxes and Shared Drive to Shared Drive for twenty-two terabytes. Migration lead Amara Sissoko opened the kickoff call by telling the customer the thing they least expected: same platform to same platform is not the easy case, and the reason is identity. In a cross-platform move, every object is translated and the team expects to map users. In a same-platform move the objects look identical, so everybody assumes the identities are too, and they are not: a user in the source tenant and the same person in the destination are different accounts with different identifiers, and every permission, every ownership record and every shared drive membership refers to the source identifier. Lattimer had two hundred and eleven people whose corporate email had changed at some point in twelve years, and whose accounts therefore matched by name but not by address. Amara insisted on a reconciled mapping file signed by the customer HR team before a single object moved, which took nine days and was the most valuable nine days of the project. Even so, fourteen accounts had no match at all: contractors who had left, whose files were owned by nobody. Those went to an orphan drive owned by an IT service account, with a review date that has passed once already. The Mail side produced the neat mirror of the same problem. Delegates and shared mailbox permissions also refer to identifiers, so a mailbox whose delegate was one of the two hundred and eleven arrived with the delegate silently missing rather than visibly broken. Amara had the team run a permission comparison after cutover rather than trusting the migration report, which found sixty-three such cases. Her closing line in the post-mortem was that on a same-platform move the migration is the easy half and the identity reconciliation is the project.'),

    ('Merrow Energy: Copy, Prove, Delete',
     'Merrow Energy sold one of its three divisions, and the sale agreement required every record belonging to that division to be delivered to the buyer and then removed from Merrow systems by a date written into the contract. Two migrations carried it out. Teams to Chat, because the buyer runs Google, moving four hundred users conversations; and SPO to SPO, a tenant-to-tenant move of ninety thousand documents. Consultant Nadia Farrell described the work as three jobs rather than two: copy, prove, delete. The copy was uneventful and took eleven days. The proving was the project. Merrow legal team wanted evidence that content had gone; the buyer wanted evidence it had arrived intact; and the two evidence sets had to reconcile, because a mismatch of even a hundred documents would delay the completion payment. Nadia produced a manifest before deletion, had the buyer confirm receipt against it, and only then ran the removal, capturing a second manifest afterwards. A mismatch appeared anyway: the buyer count was four hundred and six lower than hers. Engineer Tomas Lindqvist assumed a failed transfer and prepared to recopy. Nadia checked first. All four hundred and six were duplicate documents that the destination had merged on arrival, which is its documented behaviour and was written in the design. A recopy would have produced the same number and cost two days. On the Message side the equivalent question had no such tidy answer. Teams channel conversations moved, but one-to-one chats between a seller employee and a buyer employee belong, in a sense, to both, and no clause in the agreement addressed them. Merrow lawyers accepted a rule that Nadia proposed on the call: one-to-one chats migrate only where both participants transfer with the division. Nine hundred conversations were therefore left behind, and the completion payment was released on time.')
    ) AS v(title, script)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l3-listening-bank-v3');

